   321	**3.2** **Metrics of Resource Usage**
   322	
   323	
   324	As a basis for characterizing the computational and monetary cost
   325	of executing workflows, we define and compute several metrics.
   326	
   327	
   328	_VM time._ To capture the wall-clock time of each executed job, we
   329	compute _VM time_ as the time between the start and the termination
   330	of a job, which equals the time that the VM executing the job has
   331	been up and running for this purpose. Because each VM executes
   332	only a single job at a time, this metric accurately captures the
   333	time that a job consumes. The VM time of a workflow run is the
   334	sum of the VM time of each job triggered within the workflow.
   335	Unless mentioned otherwise, we give VM time in minutes, while
   336	normalizing fractions to 60 seconds, i.e., 3.25 minutes means 3
   337	minutes and 15 seconds.
   338	
   339	
   340	_VM cost._ As an estimate of the monetary cost imposed by executing workflows, we compute _VM cost_ as the amount of US dollars
   341	charged by GitHub according to its pricing policy. The base price
   342	per VM time is $0.008. As different operating systems and hardware
   343	impose different costs, GitHub multiplies the consumed VM time
